weave-knee trial v2
fanout: 10
seed: 2
weaver: claude-sonnet-5
judge: claude-sonnet-5
quality: 3
narrative:
  Mirgri is a ring of ten modules, and the ring closes on itself: Nexvar the vault delegates its overflow to Saxvar the index, Saxvar streams its evictions to Varbra the sampler, Varbra signals backpressure to Osthul the scheduler, Osthul reports missed heartbeats to Felost the registry, Felost publishes its sweep verdicts to Varost the monitor, Varost hands its sealed ledger to Zindor the broker, Zindor delegates its overflow to Pambra the ledger, Pambra streams its evictions to Kelfel the cache, Kelfel signals backpressure to Tolsax the throttle, and Tolsax reports missed heartbeats back to Nexvar — a single closed loop touching every module exactly once before returning to its start.

  Nexvar sits at the head of that loop as the vault: it refuses new writes while its own sweep runs, it evicts anything left unclaimed after 11 ticks, and it will not act until 3 of its peers countersign. What it cannot absorb it hands downstream to Saxvar. Saxvar, the index, also refuses writes during its sweep, but it evicts far sooner — after just 7 ticks — and it keeps only digests of what passes through it, never the bodies; whatever it discards during that fast eviction is streamed onward to Varbra.

  Varbra, the sampler, likewise refuses writes mid-sweep and keeps digests rather than bodies, matching Saxvar's discipline of never holding full payloads. But Varbra's caution shows up differently: a failed handoff gets retried up to 4 times before being parked, and rather than passing eviction data forward it turns pressure back on the pipeline, signaling backpressure to Osthul.

  Osthul, the scheduler, needs the heaviest consensus in this stretch of the ring — 4 of its peers must countersign before it acts, one more than Nexvar requires. It also refuses writes during its sweep, and it answers every read from its newest snapshot only, never an older one. When it senses trouble, it reports missed heartbeats to Felost, mirroring the same heartbeat relation that will eventually close the loop at Nexvar.

  Felost, the registry, evicts after 7 ticks — the same short window as Saxvar — and refuses writes during its own sweep; it also retries a failed handoff up to 4 times, matching Varbra's tolerance, before parking it. Its output is a sweep verdict, published to Varost.

  Varost, the monitor, refuses writes while sweeping and keeps digests only, like Saxvar and Varbra before it, but its eviction window sits between the two extremes seen so far: 9 ticks, longer than Saxvar's or Felost's 7 but shorter than Nexvar's 11. Once Varost seals its ledger, it hands it to Zindor.

  Zindor, the broker, answers reads only from its newest snapshot, as Osthul does, and requires only 3 countersigning peers to act — the same threshold as Nexvar, and one fewer than Osthul's 4. It retries failed handoffs up to 4 times, joining Varbra and Felost at that same ceiling, before delegating its overflow onward to Pambra.

  Pambra, the ledger, refuses writes during its sweep, answers reads from its newest snapshot only like Zindor and Osthul, and shares the 4-retry ceiling with Varbra, Felost, and Zindor — four of the ring's ten modules converge on that same retry limit. Pambra streams its evictions to Kelfel.

  Kelfel, the cache, demands the ring's strictest consensus of all — 5 countersigning peers, higher than Osthul's 4 or Nexvar's and Zindor's 3 — and evicts after 7 ticks, joining Saxvar and Felost as the third module sharing that same short floor. Its retries cap at 3, one below the 4-retry cluster, before it signals backpressure to Tolsax.

  Tolsax, the throttle, is the ring's least patient module: a single retry before parking, far below Kelfel's 3 or the 4-retry cluster, though it holds the longest eviction window of all at 12 ticks and, like Saxvar, Varbra, and Varost, keeps only digests. It is Tolsax that reports the missed heartbeat back to Nexvar, closing the ring — meaning the vault's own gate is fed by the module built to give up on a handoff the fastest, while every countersign threshold in the loop (3, 4, 3, 5) and every eviction floor (11, 7, 9, 7, 7, 12 ticks) coexist across a single continuous circuit with no exit.
